WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:02.919
Do you have questions after watching the sequel
to The Boy?

00:00:02.919 --> 00:00:04.170
Because we certainly do.

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:05.170
A lot of them.

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:08.740
To answer them all, here's the ending of Brahms:
The Boy II explained.

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:09.720
Spoilers ahead.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:15.519
2016's The Boy was a middling horror flick
redeemed by one heck of a surprise ending.

00:00:15.519 --> 00:00:19.310
After spending most of its runtime convincing
both its characters and the audience that

00:00:19.310 --> 00:00:23.630
the movie's creepy doll, Brahms, was possessed
by the spirit of a dead child, The Boy pulled

00:00:23.630 --> 00:00:27.189
out the rug from under everyone: The doll
really was just a doll.

00:00:27.189 --> 00:00:30.769
The film's real villain was the real-life
Brahms Heelshire, who didn't actually die

00:00:30.769 --> 00:00:34.660
but hid in the walls of his family's home
for decades, living vicariously through the

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:36.170
porcelain plaything.

00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:38.250
"It's Brahms.

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:40.870
It can't be."

00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:45.350
It's a delightfully absurd and oddly elegant
twist, and Brahms: The Boy II desperately

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:47.420
wants to recapture the same magic.

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:52.250
As the title implies, Brahms the doll, not
the man has somehow returned for the sequel,

00:00:52.250 --> 00:00:54.460
and a lot of the movie hinges on that mystery.

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:58.110
After all, we saw the original Brahms die
at the end of The Boy, so who's behind this

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:00.560
new set of supernatural shenanigans?

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:05.400
For better or worse, Brahms: The Boy II completely
undoes the original film's ending.

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:09.639
The sequel wastes no time showing us that,
despite what The Boy said, Brahms is much,

00:01:09.639 --> 00:01:11.509
much more than a porcelain doll.

00:01:11.509 --> 00:01:14.899
Almost as soon as he appears, Brahms begins
moving of his own free will.

00:01:14.899 --> 00:01:18.770
He tells his new companion, the young trauma
victim Jude, things that the kid couldn't

00:01:18.770 --> 00:01:19.780
possibly know.

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:22.640
He flips over tables and rips up teddy bears.

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:25.740
However, it's not until the end that we learn
the whole truth.

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:29.669
As Jude's mother Liza, played by Katie Holmes,
learns, the porcelain doll is the host for

00:01:29.669 --> 00:01:32.959
a malevolent entity that has been tearing
families apart for centuries.

00:01:32.959 --> 00:01:36.869
Typically, a child adopts the doll, then commits
a heinous murder.

00:01:36.869 --> 00:01:39.880
At the end of The Boy II, we even see Brahms'
real face.

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:43.639
Jude ends up throwing the the doll in the
fire, but that's not the end of Brahms.

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:46.920
As it turns out, evil toys aren't that easy
to kill.

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:51.400
After Jude, Sean, and Liza return to London,
the film unleashes a final twist.

00:01:51.429 --> 00:01:55.399
Alone in his room, Jude puts on a porcelain
mask modeled after Brahm's face and looks

00:01:55.399 --> 00:01:56.520
in the mirror.

00:01:56.520 --> 00:02:00.340
Jude thinks Brahms will be very happy in his
new home, he says as long as Jude's parents

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:02.460
remember to follow the rules.

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:07.030
If you're not sure what's happening here,
recall Joseph's big info dump a little earlier.

00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:08.399
Brahms knew that Jude was coming.

00:02:08.399 --> 00:02:11.209
He told Joseph to bury the doll where Jude
could find him.

00:02:11.209 --> 00:02:14.299
"He knew your boy would find him."

00:02:14.299 --> 00:02:17.400
Joseph also says that Brahms and Jude will
soon "become one."

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:20.990
The implication seems to be that Brahms knew
that his doll body would be destroyed and

00:02:20.990 --> 00:02:22.930
made plans to possess Jude.

00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:27.400
During Brahms: The Boy II's climax, when Jude
is pointing the shotgun at Liza, the possession

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:29.299
might've already taken place.

00:02:29.299 --> 00:02:31.870
It's clearly Brahms, not Jude, who's really
speaking.

00:02:31.870 --> 00:02:36.689
Of course, there's another possible reading
maybe Brahms really is gone, and poor, mentally

00:02:36.689 --> 00:02:41.189
fragile Jude has simply developed a split
personality based on the creature but either

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:43.560
way, Brahms lives on through the boy.

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:46.800
So now we know what Brahms does, but what
does he want?

00:02:46.819 --> 00:02:48.610
Quite simply, to be loved.

00:02:48.610 --> 00:02:52.370
In The Boy, Brahms forces the Heelshires,
and later Greta, to follow a strict set of

00:02:52.370 --> 00:02:53.370
rules.

00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:57.070
In The Boy II, Brahms makes Liza, Jude, and
Sean follow similar rules.

00:02:57.070 --> 00:03:00.909
In both movies, he only lashes out when he's
ignored or abused or when people threaten

00:03:00.909 --> 00:03:02.939
to separate him from his loved ones.

00:03:02.939 --> 00:03:06.720
As long as Brahms is treated with love and
respect, he's docile.

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:10.080
"Boo!"

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:13.799
There has to be a reason why Brahms only latches
on to families, after all.

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:17.240
He wants to be part of one, and he'll stop
anyone who gets in his way.

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:21.880
Sure, he's a creepy doll, tiny demon, and
serial killer, but hidden in the two The Boy

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:26.540
movies is the implication that maybe, just
maybe, Brahms can also function as a force

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:27.540
for good.

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:30.069
See, Brahms tends to latch on to trauma victims.

00:03:30.069 --> 00:03:34.349
In the first movie, Greta is rattled by an
abusive relationship and mourning a miscarriage.

00:03:34.349 --> 00:03:38.530
The Boy II opens with a home invasion that
leaves Liza a paranoid wreck and traumatizes

00:03:38.530 --> 00:03:41.239
Jude so badly that he won't even speak.

00:03:41.239 --> 00:03:44.860
Joseph says that Brahms likes to prey on the
mentally weak, but ironically, all of the

00:03:44.860 --> 00:03:49.040
characters actually get better through their
association with Brahms, not worse.

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:52.930
Greta essentially adopts the doll once she
starts to believe that he's alive, using him

00:03:52.930 --> 00:03:55.189
as a surrogate for the child she lost.

00:03:55.189 --> 00:03:58.010
The trend continues in Brahms: The Boy II.

00:03:58.010 --> 00:04:02.209
Jude regains his ability to speak thanks to
his bond with Brahms, and comes to rely on

00:04:02.209 --> 00:04:04.069
Brahms as a source of protection.

00:04:04.069 --> 00:04:07.159
After her encounter with Brahms, Liza learns
how to stick up for herself.

00:04:07.159 --> 00:04:10.830
She gets her family back, and her nightmares
and panic attacks disappear.

00:04:10.830 --> 00:04:15.090
Brahms might have a violent streak, but it
turns out that he's also a pretty good therapist.

00:04:15.090 --> 00:04:16.150
Just don't make him angry.

00:04:16.150 --> 00:04:19.889
"Be good to him and he'll be good to you.

00:04:19.889 --> 00:04:21.800
Be bad to him...oh!"

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:26.600
The Boy II's big retcon explains the original
movie's biggest unanswered question: Why did

00:04:26.630 --> 00:04:29.530
Brahms Heelshire kill his best friend in the
first place?

00:04:29.530 --> 00:04:33.340
According to The Boy, Brahms and a young girl
named Emily Cribbs were close friends who

00:04:33.340 --> 00:04:34.949
played together all the time.

00:04:34.949 --> 00:04:39.660
Unfortunately, Emily was murdered when Brahms
was eight, and the boy was the prime suspect.

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:43.169
In order to protect their son, the Heelshires
faked Brahms' death in a fire and hid him

00:04:43.169 --> 00:04:46.630
in the walls of their house, where he lived
for more than two decades.

00:04:46.630 --> 00:04:50.230
But The Boy never says why young Brahms decided
to get rid of Emily.

00:04:50.230 --> 00:04:53.920
The implication is that Brahms was just a
bad little boy, but thanks to Brahms: The

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:57.000
Boy II, we now have a much more concrete explanation.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:59.560
It wasn't Brahms' idea to kill Emily at all.

00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:03.140
It was the entity lurking inside that sinister
doll who wanted her dead.

00:05:03.140 --> 00:05:06.040
Like Jude, Brahms was just the creature's
instrument.

00:05:06.040 --> 00:05:09.000
The Boy was clearly designed as a one-and-done.

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:11.190
By the time the movie is over, the bad guy
is dead.

00:05:11.190 --> 00:05:12.460
The heroes have escaped.

00:05:12.460 --> 00:05:13.900
The doll is just a doll.

00:05:13.900 --> 00:05:15.860
There really isn't anywhere else to go.

00:05:15.860 --> 00:05:19.210
That's fine for a single movie, but if you're
trying to build a series it's a non-starter.

00:05:19.210 --> 00:05:23.849
Brahms: The Boy II might've sacrificed a lot
of the original's charm in order to set up

00:05:23.849 --> 00:05:27.800
future installments of the Boy franchise,
but now that the work is done the series could

00:05:27.800 --> 00:05:28.810
go anywhere.

00:05:28.810 --> 00:05:32.570
The most obvious place to take Brahms: The
Boy II's sequel would be to follow Jude in

00:05:32.570 --> 00:05:35.680
his creepy porcelain mask as he becomes a
real killer.

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:37.030
The Boy could also look backwards.

00:05:37.030 --> 00:05:41.150
As the sequel established, Brahms has been
causing havoc for hundreds of years.

00:05:41.150 --> 00:05:44.470
Plenty of families succumbed to Brahms' whims
before the Heelshires.

00:05:44.470 --> 00:05:47.240
Lakeshore Entertainment could pump out prequels
for years.

00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:49.860
On the other hand, maybe keeping it simple
is best.

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:53.260
Just have another unsuspecting family find
a creepy doll out in the woods and you're

00:05:53.260 --> 00:05:56.200
well on your way to Boy Story 3.

